/// Create the appropriate UI based on context, with GitHub Actions support.
pub fn create_ui_with_options(
    interactive: bool,
    mode: OutputMode,
    github_actions: bool,
) -> Box<dyn UserInterface> {
    if github_actions {
        Box::new(GitHubActionsUI::new(mode))
    } else if interactive && Term::stdout().is_term() {
        Box::new(TerminalUI::new(mode))
    } else {
        Box::new(NonInteractiveUI::new(mode))
    }
}
```

However, to avoid a breaking change on the existing `create_ui()` function (which may be used in tests and by external consumers), keep the existing `create_ui()` unchanged and wire the GitHub Actions path in `main.rs` directly. This is simpler and more localized:

**Update `src/main.rs`:**

```rust
use bivvy::shell::{is_ci, is_github_actions};
use bivvy::ui::{create_ui, GitHubActionsUI, OutputMode};

// ...in main():

// Determine if GitHub Actions mode is active
let use_github_actions = cli.github_actions || is_github_actions();

// Create UI
let mut ui: Box<dyn UserInterface> = if use_github_actions {
    Box::new(GitHubActionsUI::new(output_mode))
} else {
    create_ui(is_interactive, output_mode)
};
```

This approach:
- Preserves the existing `create_ui()` API for all other callers
- Gives the `--github-actions` flag precedence (explicit opt-in)
- Falls back to auto-detection via `is_github_actions()`
- When `GITHUB_ACTIONS` is set, `is_ci()` is also true, so `is_interactive` will already be `false`

